1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,350
Rebooting a server sounds simple, until it is a production box with users on it.

2
00:00:05,370 --> 00:00:10,257
The right way to restart, or power off, is calm, planned, and can be cancelled.

3
00:00:10,727 --> 00:00:14,227
In this video: boot, reboot, and shut down a system normally.

4
00:00:18,747 --> 00:00:21,330
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

5
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:24,510
Boot, reboot, and shut down a system normally.

6
00:00:24,980 --> 00:00:29,416
We'll see how long a machine has been up, schedule a shutdown and cancel it,

7
00:00:29,436 --> 00:00:33,110
reboot for real, and confirm that the system came back healthy.

8
00:00:42,630 --> 00:00:46,045
Three actions. Boot: the machine starts up. Reboot:

9
00:00:46,065 --> 00:00:49,547
it shuts down cleanly, then starts again. Power off:

10
00:00:49,567 --> 00:00:52,039
it shuts down cleanly, and stays off.

11
00:01:02,509 --> 00:01:06,518
Normally, you use systemctl reboot, or systemctl poweroff.

12
00:01:06,538 --> 00:01:10,268
The shutdown command does the same, and can also wait:

13
00:01:10,288 --> 00:01:14,713
shutdown -r for reboot, shutdown -h for power off, then a delay.

14
00:01:26,233 --> 00:01:31,568
Start by looking. uptime -p prints how long the machine has been up, in plain words.

15
00:01:36,038 --> 00:01:38,898
who -b shows the date and time of the last boot.

16
00:01:44,418 --> 00:01:49,491
Now a planned shutdown. shutdown -h +10 means: power off in ten minutes.

17
00:01:49,511 --> 00:01:53,876
The system prints the exact time, and tells you how to cancel.

18
00:01:53,896 --> 00:01:56,281
Everyone logged in gets a warning.

19
00:02:02,751 --> 00:02:07,445
Changed your mind? shutdown -c cancels it. Nothing happens to the machine.

20
00:02:12,965 --> 00:02:16,523
systemctl is-system-running tells you the overall state.

21
00:02:16,543 --> 00:02:19,014
Right after a boot it can say starting.

22
00:02:19,034 --> 00:02:23,998
The --wait option waits until startup has finished, and then prints the state,

23
00:02:24,018 --> 00:02:25,084
normally running.

24
00:02:29,554 --> 00:02:34,386
We do not power off in this lab, because that would end the virtual machine.

25
00:02:34,406 --> 00:02:38,728
But the idea is the same, as systemctl poweroff, or shutdown -h now.

26
00:02:46,248 --> 00:02:49,684
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

27
00:02:51,204 --> 00:02:52,536
Now the real reboot.

28
00:02:52,556 --> 00:02:57,269
systemctl reboot stops the services cleanly, and restarts the machine.

29
00:02:57,289 --> 00:02:59,568
The connection drops, as expected.

30
00:03:03,038 --> 00:03:07,882
After the boot, uptime is back to almost zero, who -b shows a new boot time,

31
00:03:07,902 --> 00:03:09,546
and the system is running.

32
00:03:20,066 --> 00:03:24,638
Your turn. Schedule a power off in twenty minutes, then cancel it.

33
00:03:24,658 --> 00:03:26,586
Reboot the machine normally.

34
00:03:26,606 --> 00:03:29,718
After it is back, make a folder called lab20,

35
00:03:29,738 --> 00:03:33,614
and save the output of who -b in a file called boot.txt.

36
00:03:40,084 --> 00:03:42,901
Run the checker, and try before you open the solution.

37
00:03:48,421 --> 00:03:51,622
Next up: boot systems into different targets manually.

38
00:03:52,092 --> 00:03:54,995
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
